    "_comment_coupled_relay": "robu sells relay boards from a category literally named \"Optocoupler / Light-Coupled Relay Modules\". The optical isolation is a feature of the board, not the product, and `optocoupler` is the longer key — so 68 relay modules shelved as bare optocoupler ICs, in the wrong drawer. The key has to be longer than `optocoupler` to win, which is why it names three words.",
    "coupled relay module": "industrial-electricals.drives-automation.industrial-relays",
    "_comment_ic_shelves": "robu splits its ICs six ways and five of those had nowhere to go but Logic ICs — 8,200 rows in one leaf. Weakening the buckets was tried and rejected: letting titles decide sent 139 \"LED Driver IC\" rows to the LED leaf, 42 L293D stepper-driver chips to NEMA 17 motors and 21 LCD driver ICs to displays, all of which are the driver, not the thing driven, and two of which cross an HSN drawer. The shelves were the problem, not the matcher, so the shelves moved. \"Switching IC\" goes here too: ~400 of its 481 rows are USB bridges and encoder/decoder pairs, and the ~65 switching regulators in it are less wrong here than in Logic ICs.",
    "rhcp": "drones-parts.fpv-system.antennas",
    "lhcp": "drones-parts.fpv-system.antennas",
    "lollipop": "drones-parts.fpv-system.antennas",
    "_comment_fpv_antenna": "Circular polarisation is an FPV idea. `rhcp`, `lhcp` and `lollipop` appear on 46 rows across all four suppliers and every one is a 5.8GHz video antenna, so they can be trusted absolutely — but they are all shorter than `antenna`, and longest-wins handed every one to the generic RF leaf. Hence priorityKeys. Two candidates were measured and rejected, which is the point of measuring: `rp-sma` appears on 63 rows of which 41 are ordinary LoRa and WiFi antennas that merely use that connector, and `pagoda` — a real FPV antenna shape — is ALSO a hose-barb shape, so it promoted two CPU water-cooling blocks into the drone drawer. It was in this list for one remap. Three Pagoda antennas now fall to the generic RF leaf instead, which is the cheaper mistake.",
